== store_clean_attack
store-clean: empty: no store stands — clean reclaims nothing and exits 0
store-clean: absent: a store directory holds no body — clean seals nothing
store-clean: reclaim: 10 revisions stand before clean
store-clean: all-live: a window of 100 evicts nothing
store-clean: reclaim: a window of two evicts the oldest revision and the live answer stands
store-clean: travel: the kept revision 96 answers its rows from its own body
store-clean: gone: the evicted revision 82 answers nothing
store-clean: show: a non-number refuses (exit 2), a missing revision refuses (exit 1), a negative limit is off
store-clean: reader: a check beside clean — both answers stand
store-clean: torn: a writer killed between a frame and its marker, then clean
store-clean: hold: a writer holds the lock — clean says it skipped and nothing is corrupted
store-clean: age: a one-second age evicts history older than it
store-clean: size: a 1 MiB ceiling trims the history of a larger store
store-clean: 13 held
status 0
== store_attacks
store-attacks: §6.8 one store: a cold check leaves .avra-cache/{store,obj,bin} and nothing else
store-attacks: §6.8 one open: a warm no-op check derives nothing and opens the store once
store-attacks: S5 validity: a warm check sees an edit through the store
store-attacks: §6.8 kill mid-append: a writer killed between a frame and its marker leaves every earlier frame
store-attacks: §6.8 two writers: two checks at once both answer the oracle, and both their segments stand
store-attacks: §6.8 compaction contended: the process without the lock says it did not compact, and both answer the oracle
store-attacks: §6.8 a flipped byte: one in a live segment's last frame, one in a sealed body — each seen as torn, never read
store-attacks: §6.8 another compiler, same producer: its module records are read, and the answer is the cold one
store-attacks: §6.8 another compiler, another format: a segment written under another pack format is never read, and the check recomputes
store-attacks: §6.8 another compiler (a one-query rebuild recomputes that query only)
store-attacks: FAILED — the families are keyed per FAMILY (sig by its producer's code hashes, every other by the compiler's print), so a rebuild that changed one query rewrites the whole store — the per-query recompute is S2's, DB 05b's receipt
store-attacks: §6.8 another compiler, another C: a compiler whose runtime C differs reads none of the first one's module records
store-attacks: §6.8 claim conflict: two values for one key are reported, naming both digests and their writers (S7)
store-attacks: 1 FAILED, 12 held
